<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Over the pole or along the parallel</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\(P({@phi@}^\circ\,\mathrm{[[if test="nh"]]N[[else]]S[[/if]]}, {@l1@}^\circ\,\mathrm{E})\) and \(Q({@phi@}^\circ\,\mathrm{[[if test="nh"]]N[[else]]S[[/if]]}, {@l2@}^\circ\,\mathrm{W})\) are on opposite meridians. Kamau says: <q>\(P\) and \(Q\) have the same latitude, so the shortest way from \(P\) to \(Q\) is along their parallel.</q></p><p>(a) Find the distance from \(P\) to \(Q\) along the parallel, to the nearest nautical mile.</p><p>[[input:ans1]] nm [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p><p>(b) Find the distance from \(P\) to \(Q\) along the great circle through the [[if test="nh"]]North Pole[[else]]South Pole[[/if]], in nautical miles.</p><p>[[input:ans2]] nm [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p><p>(c) Which statement is right?</p><p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) The longitudes add to \({@l1@}^\circ + {@l2@}^\circ = 180^\circ\), so \(P\) and \(Q\) are half way round their parallel:</p><p>\[180 \times 60 \times \cos {@phi@}^\circ = {@dispdp(float(par),1)@} \approx {@floor(par+1/2)@} \text{ nm}.\]</p><p>(b) The two meridians are opposite, so together they form a great circle through the poles. From \(P\) to the [[if test="nh"]]North Pole[[else]]South Pole[[/if]] is \(90^\circ - {@phi@}^\circ = {@90-phi@}^\circ\), and the same again on to \(Q\): \({@90-phi@}^\circ + {@90-phi@}^\circ = {@180-2*phi@}^\circ = {@pl@}'\), so \({@pl@}\) nm.</p><p>(c) The route over the pole is shorter. A parallel is a small circle, and the shortest path between two points on the surface follows the great circle through them, so Kamau is wrong.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[cosd(x) := cos(x*%pi/180);
sind(x) := sin(x*%pi/180);
atand(x) := 180*atan(x)/%pi;
asind(x) := 180*asin(x)/%pi;
two(v) := if v < 10 then sconcat("0", string(v)) else string(v);
hm(m) := block([q], q: mod(m, 1440), sconcat(two(floor(q/60)), ":", two(mod(q, 60))));
dmx(m) := sconcat(string(floor(m/60)), "^\\circ", two(mod(m, 60)), "'");
/* Design: a claim to criticise, comparing the route along a parallel with the route over the pole. Curated list of ten latitudes, one seed per entry. Do not use 30, 45 or 60 degrees: there the diagnostic values collide. Source: adapted from SMP Book 5 (Metric), chapter 3, Exercise E question 3. */
L: [[15,"N",25],[25,"S",60],[35,"N",110],[40,"S",145],[50,"N",15],[55,"S",90],[65,"N",160],[75,"S",35],[85,"N",125],[10,"S",70]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
phi: L[ix][1];
ns: L[ix][2];
nh: is(ns="N");
l1: L[ix][3];
l2: 180-l1;
par: 10800*cosd(phi);
pl: 60*(180-2*phi);
/* diagnostics */
nc: 10800; ps: 10800*sind(phi); full: 21600*cosd(phi);
hp: 60*(90-phi); tp: 120*phi; op: 60*(180-phi);
opt3: [[1, false, "Kamau is right: points on the same parallel are joined most directly along it."], [2, true, "Kamau is wrong: the route over the pole is part of a great circle, and it is shorter."], [3, false, "Kamau is right: the two routes are the same length."], [4, false, "Kamau is wrong: the parallel route is longer only because it crosses the \(180^\circ\) meridian."]];
]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
